Chapter 3: Discussions

3.1 Introduction

This chapter discusses how the Tic-Tac-Toe game will be designed and developed using Java.
It explains the design approach, the tools that will be used and how the different features of the game are expected to work.
It also discusses the programming concepts that will be applied during development.

3.2 Design Approach

The project uses an object-oriented programming approach.
This means that the program is divided into classes, where each class is responsible for a specific part of the game.
This helps to make the program easier to understand, organise and maintain.

The game is designed as a desktop application with a simple interface that allows users to play without difficulty.
The customary 3*3 Tic-Tac-Toe board is used, with X and O representing the two players.

3.3 Graphical User Interface

The graphical user interface (GUI) uses Java Swing to make the game simple and easy to use.
The application displays a customary 3*3 Tic-Tac-Toe board where players place their symbols, X and O.
It provides two game modes: Two-Player Mode and AI Mode.
In AI mode, users can also select the computer's difficulty level.

The interface includes a scoreboard that tracks the scores of X, O and ties. 
It also displays the game status and provides a New Game button and a Reset Score button to help users manage their games.

3.4 Game Modes

The application will support two game modes.

Two-player modes
In this mode, two players will play against each other using the same computer.
The first player will use X while the second player will use O.
Players will take turns selecting empty spaces on the board until one player wins or the game ends in a draw.

Player-versus-computer mode

In this mode, one player will play against a computer-controlled opponent.
The human player will use one token, while the computer will use the other.
After the human player makes a move, the computer will automatically select an available position on the board.

3.5 Artificial Intelligence

The game will include an artificial intelligence (AI) opponent to allow users to play when another human player is unavailable.
The AI will have different difficulty levels to provide players with different challenges.

The easier difficulty level will allow the computer to make simpler moves, while the harder difficulty level will make use of better decision-making strategies to increase the challenge for the human player.
This feature will make the game more interesting because users will be able to choose how challenging they want their opponent to be.

3.6 Game Logic and Score Tracking

Game logic refers to the rules and instructions that control how the game works.
It will manage player turns, check whether a selected position is empty and prevent players from placing their tokens in positions that are already occupied.
After each move, the application will check whether a player has won.

A player will win by placing three matching tokens in a horizontal, vertical or diagonal line.
If all nine positions are occupied and neither player has won, the game will be declared a draw.
The application will also include a scoring system to keep track of the players' results.
This will allow users to see how many games have been won or lost.

3.7 Development Tools and Programming Concepts

The project is developed using Java as the programming language and IntelliJ IDEA as the development environment.
Java Swing is used to create the graphical user interface.

The project applies object-oriented programming concepts such as classes, objects, constructors and access modifiers. 
These concepts help to organise the program and control how different parts of the application work together.

Control statements, loops and conditional statements are also used.
For example, conditional statements assist in checking winning combinations, while loops are useful when checking the position on the game board.

3.8 Testing and Evaluation

Testing was carried out during the development of the application to check whether the different features work correctly.
The group tested both game modes, the AI difficulty levels, player turns, winning combinations, draw conditions and the scoring system.

The graphical user interface was also tested to make sure that the buttons and other components respond correctly when users interact with them.
Any errors identified during the test were corrected.
The main aim of testing was to make sure that the game works as expected and is easy for users to play.

Chapter 4: Results/ Analysis

4.1 Introduction

This chapter presents the results obtained from the development and testing of the Tic-Tac-Toe game. 
It discusses the performance of the application and evaluates whether the implemented features meet the project requirements.

4.2 Application Results

The project results in a functional Tic-Tac-Toe game developed using Java Swing.
The application provides a graphical interface that allows users to play using the traditional 3*3 board.

The game includes two-player mode and player-versus-computer mode.
It also provides different AI difficulty levels, a scoreboard and options to start a new game or reset the scores.

4.3 Testing Results

The group tested the different features of the application to ensure that they work correctly.
The testing results showed that all the implemented features function as expected.

The following features were tested:

Two-Player Mode: Both players can take turns playing against each other.
AI Mode: The player can compete against the computer.
AI Difficulty levels: The Easy, Medium and Hard levels work correctly.
Winning Conditions: The game correctly identifies the winner when three matching symbols appear in a row, column or diagonal.
Draw Conditions: The game correctly identifies when a round ends in a draw.
Scoreboard: The scores for X, O and ties update correctly after each round.
New Game: The game allows users to start another round.
Reset Score: The scoreboard resets when the button is selected.

4.4 Analysis of Results

Based on the testing results, the application meets the main requirements of the project.
All the implemented features work correctly, and users can play in both game modes without difficulties.

The graphical interface makes the game easy to understand and interact with.
The AI difficulty levels provide different challenges, while the scoreboard allows users to keep track of their performance.

Overall, the results show that Java and Java Swing provide the necessary tools to develop a functional desktop-based Tic-Tac-Toe game.

